{c}

= a
{parent=2007/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P3/a}

**The printed product omits the node with index zero.** The correct cardinal polynomial is
$$
L_k(x)=\prod_{\substack{0\le i\le n\\i\ne k}}\frac{x-x_i}{x_k-x_i}.
$$
For example, with two nodes zero and one, values zero and one, and both derivative data zero, the printed index range gives $L_1=1$ and its displayed interpolant is the constant one, which fails the value condition at zero. Thus the source formula cannot be proved literally.

With the corrected product, $L_k(x_j)=\delta_{kj}$. For $j\ne k$, $L_k^2$ has a double zero at $x_j$, so both <basis> functions and their derivatives vanish there. At $x_k$, put $\ell=L_k'(x_k)$. The value <basis> $H_k=L_k^2[1-2(x-x_k)\ell]$ satisfies $H_k(x_k)=1$ and $H_k'(x_k)=2\ell-2\ell=0$. The derivative <basis> $K_k=L_k^2(x-x_k)$ satisfies $K_k(x_k)=0$ and $K_k'(x_k)=1$. Both have degree at most $2n+1$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{h(x)=\sum_{k=0}^n(f_kH_k(x)+g_kK_k(x))}
$$
has every prescribed value and derivative, proving membership in the interpolation class after the required index correction. This corrected Hermite interpolant is used in the following parts.

= b
{parent=2007/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P3/b}

The difference $d=p-q$ has $d(x_i)=d'(x_i)=0$ at every node. <Rolle's theorem> supplies an additional zero of $d'$ in each of the $n$ open intervals between successive nodes. Together with the $n+1$ nodal derivative zeros, these are $2n+1$ distinct zeros of $d'$, including the two endpoints. But $d'$ is a polynomial of degree at most $2n$, so it is identically zero. Since $d(x_0)=0$, $d$ is also identically zero.

Thus **the interpolation polynomial is unique**. Once $d'=0$ identically, it indeed has arbitrarily many zeros in the open interval as requested. The initial Rolle count naturally includes the endpoints, rather than initially supplying $2n+1$ open-interval zeros. Equivalently, every node is a double root of $d$, so a nonzero difference would be divisible by the degree-$2n+2$ polynomial $\prod_i(x-x_i)^2$, exceeding its allowed degree.

= c
{parent=2007/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P3/c}

Use the corrected interpolant from part (a). The error has $e(x_i)=e'(x_i)=0$, and the chosen auxiliary function has $\phi(x_i)=\phi'(x_i)=0$ as well as $\phi(x)=0$. There are $n+2$ distinct zeros of $\phi$: all the nodes and the additional evaluation point. <Rolle's theorem> supplies $n+1$ zeros of $\phi'$ between these ordered zeros. Together with its $n+1$ nodal zeros, this gives $2n+2$ distinct derivative zeros. Repeated Rolle applications therefore give an interior $\xi$ such that $\phi^{(2n+2)}(\xi)=0$.

**The extra claim that $x$ itself must be a zero of $\phi'$ is false.** For nodes zero and one and $f(t)=t^5$, the Hermite interpolant is $3t^3-2t^2$, so $e(t)=t^2(t-1)^2(t+2)$. The auxiliary function is then $\phi(t)=t^2(t-1)^2(t-x)$, and $\phi'(x)=x^2(x-1)^2\ne0$ at any interior evaluation point. The corrected zero count above is all that is needed.

Since $h$ has degree at most $2n+1$ and $\pi^2$ is monic of degree $2n+2$, differentiating the auxiliary function at the supplied $\xi$ gives
$$
0=f^{(2n+2)}(\xi)-\frac{e(x)}{\pi(x)^2}(2n+2)!.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{e(x)=\pi(x)^2\frac{f^{(2n+2)}(\xi)}{(2n+2)!}}.
$$
At interpolation nodes the error and the prefactor are zero, so the formula remains valid there as well.

= d
{parent=2007/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P3/d}

The cubic Hermite interpolant to the exponential at zero and one is
$$
h(x)=(3-e)x^3+(2e-5)x^2+x+1.
$$
Its endpoint values are one and $e$, and its endpoint derivatives are also one and $e$. The remainder formula gives $e^x-h(x)=e^\xi x^2(x-1)^2/24$ for an interior $\xi$. On this interval $e^\xi\le e$ and $x(1-x)\le1/4$, so
$$
\boxed{|e^x-h(x)|\le\frac e{24}\left(\frac14\right)^2=\frac e{384}}.
$$

= P4
{parent=2007/P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2007/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P4/a}

Gershgorin's theorem states that every complex <eigenvalue> of a square matrix lies in at least one row disc
$$
D_i=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z-a_{ii}|\le R_i\},\qquad R_i=\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|.
$$
Let $Av=\lambda v$ with $v\ne0$, and choose an index $i$ where $|v_i|$ is maximal. Its row equation gives
$$
|\lambda-a_{ii}|\,|v_i|=\left|\sum_{j\ne i}a_{ij}v_j\right|\le\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\,|v_i|.
$$
Dividing by the positive $|v_i|$ proves $\lambda\in D_i$. This proof applies to complex eigenvectors even when the matrix is real.

= b
{parent=2007/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P4/b}

Gershgorin's second theorem states that a union of $k$ row discs separated from the union of all remaining discs contains exactly $k$ eigenvalues, counted with <algebraic multiplicity>. In particular a disc disjoint from every other disc contains exactly one <eigenvalue>. The same assertion holds for column discs by applying it to the transpose.

= c
{parent=2007/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P4/c}

Assume $0<\epsilon<1/4$, as implied by its stipulated smallness, and use the nontrivial matrix range $n\ge3$. The row radii are $\epsilon$ at one, $r=1/4+\epsilon$ at each interior index, and $1/4$ at $n$. Since $2r<1$, all discs are disjoint. The second theorem therefore gives one <eigenvalue> in each disc. Each such <eigenvalue> is real: a nonreal <eigenvalue> would have its distinct complex conjugate in the same disc, contradicting its count of one. Thus
$$
\boxed{|\lambda_1-1|\le\epsilon,\quad|\lambda_i-i|\le1/4+\epsilon\ (2\le i\le n-1),\quad|\lambda_n-n|\le1/4}.
$$
The eigenvalues are simple as well. An alternative reality argument is that a positive diagonal similarity makes this tridiagonal matrix symmetric, since its opposite off-diagonal entries have positive product.

= d
{parent=2007/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P4/d}

The transpose has first radius $1/4$, interior radii $1/4+\epsilon$, and last radius $\epsilon$. These discs are again disjoint and each has one <eigenvalue>. Intersecting with the previous enclosures therefore improves the last bound to
$$
\boxed{n-\epsilon\le\lambda_n\le n+\epsilon}.
$$
The identification with the same last <eigenvalue> is justified by separation from all the other integer-centred discs.

= e
{parent=2007/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P4/e}

Put $R=\epsilon+\delta$. Only the first row disc changes: it now has radius $R$. The other discs keep radii $r_i=1/4+\epsilon$ for $2\le i\le n-1$ and $r_n=1/4$. They remain mutually disjoint. As $R$ grows, the first disc joins an initial segment of these discs. Define $m$ as the largest index joined to it, or one if no other disc is joined. The joining condition for index $i$ is
$$
R+r_i\ge i-1.
$$
For an interior index this starts at $\delta=i-5/4-2\epsilon$, and for the final index at $\delta=n-5/4-\epsilon$. Until its joining threshold, disc $i$ contains a unique real <eigenvalue> and retains the bound $|\lambda_i-i|\le r_i$.

After the first $m$ discs have joined, the second theorem places exactly $m$ eigenvalues in their union and exactly one in each remaining disc. The cluster is bounded, for instance, by
$$
|z-1|\le\max\{R,m-1+r_m\}\quad(m\ge2),
$$
and by $|z-1|\le R$ when $m=1$. The union of the actual discs is a sharper enclosure. Once discs overlap, one cannot assign one <eigenvalue> to each individual disc merely by its centre; the theorem counts the whole separated cluster.

= f
{parent=2007/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2007/P/P4/f}

**The endpoint bounds printed in this part are false.** Take $n=3$, $\epsilon=1/100$ and $\delta=9/10$, which obey $\delta<(n-1)/2-\epsilon=99/100$. The <characteristic polynomial> is
$$
P(z)=z^3-6z^2+\frac{2199}{200}z-\frac{4837}{800}.
$$
It has $P(101/100)=-31599/10^6<0$ and $P(5/4)=441/1600>0$, so it has a real root strictly between $1.01$ and $1.25$, outside the first printed interval. There is a unique <eigenvalue> in the transpose's isolated first disc of radius $1/4$, identifying it as the first <eigenvalue>. Similarly $P(301/100)=-40999/10^6<0$ and $P(13/4)=41/64>0$ give a root above $3.01$, violating the last printed interval. The approximate three roots are $1.02675615$, $1.94385374$ and $3.02939011$.

There is nevertheless a corrected enclosure proving the claimed reality. Under the stated condition write $R=\epsilon+\delta<(n-1)/2$. The large first row disc is disjoint from the large last column disc, since their centres are $n-1$ apart and their radii are both $R$. Every <eigenvalue> lies both in the row union and in the column union. It cannot belong to both large discs, so it lies in at least one of the fixed small discs
$$
S_1:\ |z-1|\le1/4,\qquad S_i:\ |z-i|\le1/4+\epsilon\ (2\le i\le n-1),\qquad S_n:\ |z-n|\le1/4.
$$
These fixed discs are pairwise disjoint. At $\delta=0$ each contains one <eigenvalue>. Eigenvalues depend continuously, as a multiset, on the entries; throughout the <connected> allowed interval no <eigenvalue> can move between these separated fixed sets while remaining in their union. Thus each still contains one, counted with multiplicity. Complex-conjugate pairing now proves each is real. The valid conclusion is
$$
\boxed{|\lambda_1-1|\le1/4,\quad|\lambda_i-i|\le1/4+\epsilon\ (2\le i\le n-1),\quad|\lambda_n-n|\le1/4}.
$$
The source's stronger width $\epsilon$ at both endpoints does not persist after adding the corner entry; it was valid for the unperturbed matrix only.

The enclosure method is <complementary row and column Gershgorin isolation>.
